Closures are not just "functions inside functions." They are heap-allocated memory anchors that can silently sink your application’s performance.

💡 **The Analogy**
Imagine renting an entire 10,000 sq. ft. warehouse just because you left a single coffee mug inside. The landlord (Garbage Collector) can’t reclaim or bulldoze the property because you still hold the active key to that mug.

⚙️ **Under the Hood**
When an outer function finishes running, its stack frame is popped. But if an inner function escapes—via a callback, event listener, or cache—the JavaScript engine moves the outer Lexical Environment to the **heap**. 

Here is the trap: V8 often shares the same Lexical Environment context across all sibling closures in that scope. If an event listener captures *one* small variable, it can unknowingly keep massive sibling arrays or DOM references alive indefinitely.

📌 **The Trap vs. The Solution**

```javascript
// ❌ THE LEAK: 'hugeData' remains anchored on the heap
function attachListener() {
  const hugeData = new Array(1e6).fill("💥");
  const id = "btn-123";

  // Retains the parent LexicalEnvironment!
  window.addEventListener("click", () => console.log(id));
}

// ✅ THE FIX: Isolate scopes or explicitly decouple
function attachListenerFixed() {
  const id = "btn-123";
  const handler = () => console.log(id);

  window.addEventListener("click", handler);
  return () => window.removeEventListener("click", handler);
}
```

🚀 **Senior Interview Tip**
Don't just say, *"A closure preserves variables from the outer scope."* Stand out by explaining: *"Closures allocate Lexical Environments on the heap. Because engines share context records among sibling closures, long-lived callbacks can anchor unused outer variables, evading GC sweeps unless explicitly severed or isolated."*

