If you are using `throttle` to manage smooth visual updates during scroll or window resize events, you are likely dropping frames unnecessarily.

💡 **The Mental Model**
• **Debounce:** An elevator door. It waits for everyone to stop entering before finally closing. (Search autocomplete)
• **Throttle:** A turnstile letting one person through every 100ms, regardless of the queue size. (Infinite scroll API fetching)
• **requestAnimationFrame (rAF):** A conductor cueing the orchestra precisely on the beat of your screen’s hardware refresh rate. (60fps DOM animations)

⚙️ **Under the Hood**
• **Debounce** resets its internal timer on every trigger, executing the callback only after a period of silence. Ideal for saving network overhead.
• **Throttle** guarantees execution at fixed time intervals using hardware clock timers. However, browser timer callbacks (`setTimeout`) run in the macro-task queue and are completely un-synced with display hardware repaints.
• **rAF** hooks directly into the browser’s rendering pipeline—executing callbacks right before the style, layout, and paint steps. It locks perfectly to 60Hz (16.6ms) or 120Hz displays, eliminates screen tearing, and automatically pauses in inactive tabs to preserve CPU and battery.

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG: Arbitrary timer sync for UI updates
window.addEventListener('scroll', throttle(updateVisuals, 16));

// ✅ RIGHT: Render-synced UI update
let ticking = false;
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (!ticking) {
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      updateVisuals();
      ticking = false;
    });
    ticking = true;
  }
});
```

📌 **Senior Interview Tip**
Categorize rate-limiting by target layer: Use **Debounce** for user intent completion (Search), **Throttle** for periodic background tasks (Analytics/API rate limits), and **rAF** for direct visual mutations (Scroll/Resize UI updates).

