React 16 didn't just add new features—it tore out its core engine. Why? Because the JavaScript call stack doesn't care about your 60fps animations.

💡 Think of the old reconciler like a chef cooking a 100-course meal. They had to finish every dish in one continuous spree, ignoring waiting customers at the door. Fiber turns that recipe into a flexible checklist: the chef chops one onion, pauses to seat a VIP guest, and resumes chopping right where they left off.

⚙️ **Under the Hood:**
Pre-16 React used the **Stack Reconciler**. Virtual DOM updates recursively traversed the tree via the JS call stack. Because stack execution is uninterruptible, updating large component trees locked the main thread, dropping frames and causing visible jank.

**Fiber** replaced recursion with a doubly-linked list data structure. This unlocked cooperative multitasking:

1. **Render Phase (Async & Interruptible):** React traverses Fiber nodes (`child`, `sibling`, `return`). If the main thread needs to handle user input, React yields execution using a scheduler algorithm.
2. **Commit Phase (Sync & Atomic):** Once the target state is calculated, React flushes mutations to the real DOM without interruption to avoid visual tearing.

```text
// OLD: Uninterruptible Call Stack
function reconcile(node) {
  node.children.forEach(reconcile); // Trapped until deep completion
}

// FIBER: Interruptible Work Loop
while (nextUnitOfWork && !shouldYieldToBrowser()) {
  nextUnitOfWork = performUnitOfWork(nextUnitOfWork);
}
```

📌 **Senior Interview Tip:** Never say Fiber makes React "faster." Tell the interviewer Fiber is a virtualized stack frame architecture built for **scheduling priority**. It gives React time-slicing control over frame budgets.

